        "text": "The Sand-Pushing environment is an RL environment where agents must move sand particles into a goal slot. The observation space contains the Powderworld world state W \u2208 R64\u00d764\u00d720, and the action space defines the position and velocity to produce wind at (X,Y, Vx, Vy). By producing wind, agents interact with the velocity field, allowing them to push and move elements around. Wind affects the velocity field in a 10x10 area around the specified position. Reward equals the number of sand elements within the goal slot, and episodes are run for 64 timesteps.\nTo learn to control in this environment, a Stable Baselines 3 PPO agent (Raffin et al., 2021; Schulman et al., 2017) is trained over 2,500,000 environment interactions. The agent model is comprised of two convolutional layers with feature size 32 and 64 and kernel size of three, followed by two fullyconnected layers. A learning rate of 0.0003 is used, along with a batchsize of 256. An off-the-shelf RL algorithm is intentionally chosen, so experiments can focus on the impact of training tasks.\nFigure 10 highlights an agent pushing sand into the goal slot. Note that wind and velocity affect all elements present, so an agent must be careful to only push only the sand. Training tasks for the Sand-Pushing environment are generated using the same procedural generation algorithm as the world-modelling task, with the addition of an initial block of sand as well as a goal slot. A set of hand-designed test tasks (Fig 9) are presented to test various skills that a robust agent may learn.\nAs displayed in Figure 11 (Left), agents trained on a distribution of Sand-Pushing tasks can successfully generalize to the test tasks, as long as the training distribution is large enough. Similarly to world-modelling, a minimum number of training tasks is required to see generalization. However, in the RL case, agents trained on even larger numbers of training tasks still display similar levels of performance. This observation may suggest that RL representations are simple enough that additional data is unnecessary, or that reward-based learning fails to fully utilize expressive data.\nFigure 11 (Right) shows that unlike in world-modelling, increased complexity on RL tasks decreases test performance. This difference may arise from RL\u2019s need for exploration as well as its sensitivity to variance. Due to these factors, RL is highly dependent on early reward signal in order to correctly continue to explore and improve performance. RL agents trained on complex tasks also show lower validation reward, displaying that agent learning suffers with complexity (Figure 12). Additional complexity in training tasks may result in a noisy reward signal which harms agent learning.\nThe difference in how complexity affects training in Powderworld world-modelling and reinforcement learning highlights a motivating platform for further investigation. While baseline RL methods fail to scale with additional complexity and instead suffer due to variance, alternative learning techniques may better handle the learning problem and show higher generalization."
